\details{
\strong{Bandwidth.} Node \eqn{i}'s kernel bandwidth is
\eqn{b_i = \gamma\, s_i / k_i}, a multiple of the mean retained edge weight
(strength over degree).  Two settings answer different questions:
\code{gamma = 0.5} (the default here), the \emph{degree-neutral} bandwidth, at
which source-sink scores carry essentially no degree signature under symmetric
migration and which is recommended for \strong{inferring direction}; and
\code{gamma = 1}, the local mean, which keeps the contribution of degree
(hubs read as sources, leaves as sinks) and is suited to \strong{describing
connectivity}.  \code{gamma = Inf} gives the flat kernel (\eqn{w_{j|i} = 1/k_i}),
at which every quantity is set by degree alone.  Because \eqn{b_i} scales with
the edge weights, rescaling every edge weight by a constant leaves all
quantities unchanged.

